---
import Page from '../../layouts/Page.astro';
import { DEMO_URL, DOCKER_HUB_URL } from '../../site';

const faq = [
  {
    q: 'Do I need an account to try QuizDock?',
    a: `<p>No. The <a href="${DEMO_URL}" target="_blank" rel="noopener">live demo</a> opens on a shared host account, with uploads off and everything wiped every hour. On your own machine, the standalone image starts in local mode: the host types a name to take the host seat, participants join with the PIN and a nickname.</p>`,
  },
  {
    q: 'Do I need an identity provider?',
    a: `<p>No. Local mode (<code>AUTH_MODE=none</code>) is the default and needs none. It suits trusted networks: a name is not a password, so whoever types the host's name takes the seat back. When each person needs their own account, connect an OpenID Connect provider.</p>`,
  },
  {
    q: 'Can I start locally and configure OIDC later?',
    a: `<p>Yes, with the same image. Set <code>AUTH_MODE=oidc</code> and your provider's issuer and client in <code>.env</code>, then restart. Quizzes made in local mode stay in the database under their local account: an administrator hands each one over to its author's new account, from <b>Administration › Quizzes</b> (<b>Hand over</b>) or with <code>./quizdock quiz:transfer</code>. Nothing moves them automatically.</p>`,
  },
  {
    q: 'How do upgrades work?',
    a: `<p>One command on the server, in the instance's folder: <code>./quizdock upgrade X.Y.Z</code>. It backs up, pulls the image, restarts with the database migrations, waits for the health check and runs <code>doctor</code>. There is no rollback: to go back, restore the backup it took.</p>`,
  },
  {
    q: 'Can QuizDock configure itself from the browser?',
    a: `<p>Mostly. The setup wizard and the <b>Settings</b> page change the settings of levels C2 to C4: access, limits, pace, wording, look. The critical ones (database, Redis, authentication mode, ports) stay in <code>.env</code> and need a restart. Outside the wizard, web changes need <code>ADMIN_WEB_SCOPE=write</code>, and in local mode an <code>ADMIN_TOKEN</code>.</p>`,
  },
  {
    q: 'Is Docker mandatory?',
    a: `<p>In practice, yes. Every documented install uses Docker: the standalone image, or Compose through the <code>./quizdock</code> script, which needs Docker with its compose plugin and <code>curl</code>. There is no documented install without Docker. Docker Desktop on a laptop works; set <code>HOST_LAN_IPS</code> so that phones are offered the right address.</p>`,
  },
];
---

<Page
  title="Plug-and-play"
  description="Start QuizDock without an identity provider, set it up in the browser in three questions, and operate it with one script: status, doctor, backup, upgrade."
  faq={faq}
>
  <section class="page-hero">
    <h1>Easy to try. Easy to run. Easy to operate.</h1>
    <p class="lede">
      QuizDock starts without an identity provider, sets itself up from the browser, comes with sample quizzes,
      and is kept running with one script. When you need single sign-on, the same image switches to OpenID Connect.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="section">
    <div class="prose">
      <h2>Can I start without an identity provider?</h2>
      <p>
        Yes. Local mode is the default. A host opens the <b>Host area</b>, types <b>Your name</b>, chooses how long to
        <b>Keep the seat</b> (1, 4 or 24 hours, or <b>Never (until I log out)</b>; 4 hours by default) and clicks
        <b>Take the host seat</b>. Participants join with the six-digit PIN and a nickname. Nobody creates an account.
      </p>
      <p>
        There is one host seat at a time. The name is the key: typing it again on any device signs back in as that host.
        Local mode is therefore for trusted networks, such as a classroom, a team or a home. It is not a security boundary.
      </p>
      <p><a class="more-link" href="/docs/host/getting-started/">Getting started as a host →</a></p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section section-soft">
    <div class="prose">
      <h2>What does the setup wizard do?</h2>
      <p>
        A fresh instance says <b>This instance is not set up yet.</b> and offers <b>Set it up</b>. The wizard asks for a
        setup token first: whoever reaches a new instance must not own it. The token is written in the instance's logs at
        start (<code>./quizdock logs</code>). It works once and expires 24 hours after it was created.
      </p>
      <p>Then eight steps, each one changeable later:</p>
      <ul>
        <li><b>Usage</b>: three questions that set several settings at once (below).</li>
        <li><b>Health</b>: the database, Redis, the migrations, the folders, the critical variables.</li>
        <li><b>Identity</b>: the name, the language, the logo, where the feedback links lead.</li>
        <li><b>Address</b>: the addresses offered to participants, with a test from a phone.</li>
        <li><b>Access</b>: who may enter, and who administers the instance.</li>
        <li><b>Limits &amp; pace</b>, <b>Content</b> (the sample quizzes), <b>Summary</b>.</li>
      </ul>
      <p>
        <b>Finish the setup</b> closes the wizard for good. With an identity provider, finishing needs a signed-in
        administrator. An automated deployment skips the wizard with <code>./quizdock qd setup.complete</code>.
      </p>
      <p><a class="more-link" href="/docs/admin/overview/">The administration →</a></p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section">
    <div class="prose">
      <h2>What do the three setup questions set?</h2>
      <p>
        The <b>Usage</b> step shows <b>What these answers set:</b> before you click <b>Apply</b>. A variable locked by the
        operator (<code>ADMIN_LOCK</code>) is left alone. Each change is written to the audit.
      </p>
    </div>
    <div class="table-wrap">
      <table>
        <thead><tr><th>Question</th><th>Answer</th><th>What it sets</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td rowspan="2"><b>Does this instance reach the Internet?</b></td><td><b>Yes</b></td><td>Update check on; the editor links to seven free media libraries.</td></tr>
          <tr><td><b>No</b></td><td><code>UPDATE_CHECK=false</code>, <code>MEDIA_LIBRARY_LINKS=none</code>.</td></tr>
          <tr><td rowspan="3"><b>Who plays?</b></td><td><b>Colleagues, friends</b> · <b>A class, a training</b></td><td>The standard values: hosts wait up to 10 s for media to load; with an identity provider, every participant signs in.</td></tr>
          <tr><td><b>The public, a large event</b></td><td>Waits up to 20 s for media (<code>GAME_MEDIA_WAIT_S</code>). With an identity provider, lets hosts open a game to PIN and nickname (<code>ALLOW_ANONYMOUS_PARTICIPANTS=true</code>).</td></tr>
          <tr><td><b>A bit of everything, other</b></td><td>Nothing.</td></tr>
          <tr><td rowspan="2"><b>Do some participants need it easier?</b></td><td><b>No</b></td><td>3 s to read before the timer, 1 s pause once everyone answered, animations on.</td></tr>
          <tr><td><b>Yes</b></td><td>6 s to read, 2 s pause, animations off (<code>LIVE_MOTION=off</code>).</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
    <div class="prose">
      <p>
        The same presets apply from a shell, for instance <code>./quizdock qd presets.apply --internet=offline</code>.
      </p>
      <p><a class="more-link" href="/docs/admin/presets/">Presets in detail →</a></p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section section-soft">
    <div class="prose">
      <h2>Is there something to play right away?</h2>
      <p>
        Yes: three sample quizzes, about France, Taiwan and Türkiye, with their pictures (and a sound for France and Türkiye), wait in the
        templates. A host takes one from <b>Templates</b> (an empty <b>My quizzes</b> page offers <b>Browse the templates</b>)
        and gets a copy of their own. An administrator can also add them to a host's quizzes at the wizard's
        <b>Content</b> step, from <b>Administration › Quizzes</b> (<b>Add the sample quizzes</b>), or with
        <code>./quizdock samples:load</code> followed by the account.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section">
    <div class="prose">
      <h2>Can I change the configuration from the browser?</h2>
      <p>
        Yes, within limits the operator sets. Every setting has a level, shown next to it on <b>Administration › Settings</b>:
      </p>
      <ul>
        <li><b>C1</b>, critical: start-up, data or security. Shown, never changed from the web.</li>
        <li><b>C2</b>, high: who may enter, how much may be stored. Each change confirmed.</li>
        <li><b>C3</b>, normal: how the instance behaves.</li>
        <li><b>C4</b>, cosmetic: look and wording.</li>
      </ul>
      <p>
        By default <code>ADMIN_WEB_SCOPE=read</code>: the page shows every value and where it comes from (default,
        <code>.env</code>, or changed here) and changes nothing. With <code>write</code>, administrators change C2 to C4.
        <code>ADMIN_LOCK</code> names variables the web never changes. In local mode, a change also needs the
        <code>ADMIN_TOKEN</code> set in <code>.env</code>. The wizard is the exception: it sets C2 to C4 whatever the scope,
        never a locked or critical one.
 A value changed here outranks <code>.env</code>;
        <b>Take everything back</b> returns to it.
      </p>
    </div>
    <div class="shots">
      <figure>
        <img src="/screenshots/admin-settings.png" alt="The Settings page of the administration: a list of settings by category, and the selected one with its value, level C4, when it applies and its help" />
        <figcaption>Each setting with its value, its source, its level and when it applies.</figcaption>
      </figure>
    </div>
    <div class="prose"><p><a class="more-link" href="/docs/admin/settings/">Settings in detail →</a></p></div>
  </section>

  <section class="section section-soft">
    <div class="prose">
      <h2>How do I keep it running?</h2>
      <p>With one script, on the server, in the instance's folder:</p>
      <pre>./quizdock status    # containers, health, version
./quizdock doctor    # environment, database, migrations, Redis, media, OIDC
./quizdock backup    # database, media, shared templates, .env
./quizdock restore ./backups/quizdock-YYYYMMDD-HHMMSS
./quizdock upgrade X.Y.Z</pre>
      <p>
        <code>backup</code> writes a dated folder under <code>./backups/</code>; it does not schedule itself, and it leaves
        out an optional <code>branding/</code> folder. <code>upgrade</code> backs up, pulls, restarts with the migrations,
        waits for the health check and runs <code>doctor</code>. There is no rollback: going back means restoring the
        database and the media from the same backup.
      </p>
      <p>
        When a newer stable release is out, the administration shows <b>QuizDock X.Y.Z is available.</b> with the command
        to run and a link to the release notes. It installs nothing; <b>Hide until the next version</b> dismisses it.
        <code>./quizdock status</code> says it too. The server asks GitHub at most once a day; <code>UPDATE_CHECK=false</code>
        turns this off.
      </p>
      <p><a class="more-link" href="/docs/operator/cli/">The operator script →</a></p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section">
    <div class="prose">
      <h2>Can I move to single sign-on later?</h2>
      <p>
        Yes, without changing image. Set <code>AUTH_MODE=oidc</code>, <code>OIDC_ISSUER</code> and
        <code>OIDC_CLIENT_ID</code> in <code>.env</code> and restart. Hosts then need the <code>host</code> role, from the
        provider's token or granted by an administrator. Participants sign in, unless the operator allows open games.
      </p>
      <p>
        Quizzes created in local mode belong to local accounts, which nobody signs in to under OIDC. Nothing moves them
        automatically. Once the author has signed in with their new account, an administrator hands each quiz over to it:
        <b>Administration › Quizzes</b>, <b>Hand over</b>, or <code>./quizdock quiz:transfer</code> with the quiz and the
        account. The media only that quiz uses, and its history, follow it.
      </p>
      <p><a class="more-link" href="/docs/operator/oidc/">Connect an identity provider →</a></p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section">
    <div class="next">
      <a class="btn btn-primary" href={DEMO_URL} target="_blank" rel="noopener">Try the live demo ↗</a>
      <a class="btn btn-ghost" href={DOCKER_HUB_URL} target="_blank" rel="noopener">Get it on Docker Hub ↗</a>
      <a class="btn btn-ghost" href="/docs/operator/install/">Install an instance</a>
    </div>
  </section>
</Page>
